I follow what you want to do now.

You don't really care to recover or run the systems, you just want to wipe
out the drive contents.

I suggest you hook the drives up to a PC and run a program on the PC to
repeatedly write a single pattern of hex AA to the entire disk surface, say
10 passes. This could take a few hours per disk but will leave them
unrecoverable.

If you were a secure site, like NSA, you'd have them crushed.

There is no real point in getting them running on the systems in question as
NT, Tru64 nor OpenVMS will be able to destroy the data, unless you have a
running OS. Its not worth the trouble to go through the effort of installing
an OS just to wipe out the drives.

If you really do want to install and OS to wipe the rives, if I assume you
still have the SWXCR RCU (Raid Configuration Utility) then you can run that
program from either the AlphaBIOS console or the SRM console. If you use the
SRM console, you need to use a Serial Terminal, something above a VT320
series, and there are some specific setups to make it work properly.

The RCU may still be on the AlphaServer Firmware Update CD-ROM, in a utility
directory, but haven't had one in 4 years so I don't know for sure.

The SRM console has a Destructive disk drive test built-in. If you had less
than 8 disks on a SWXCR and were to use the RCU to make them JBOD (Just a
Bunch Of Drives), you could run the destructive disk drive test. It writes
to the entire surface.
I do not have an SRM Console Manual anymore, but there is a console
environment variable to be set to enable the write test. You might find it
by looking for reference in the help file.

The NT Format in my past experience has not successfully destroyed data on a
disk drive or Raid set.

The RCU is located here if you need it,

http://ftp.digital.com/cgi-bin/grep-index?rcu

It's ra200rcu.exe

I found a SRM Console reference here,

http://h18002.www1.hp.com/alphaserver/download/srm_reference.pdf

You could use the exer command example to run a script to write a pattern
across the disk surfaces, to erase all the data.

Let me know if this helps.

rtt


